From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: opencode-port <port@localhost>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:05:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] D506 code mode: console.log applies format specifiers

Upstream: console.log, info, debug, warn and error printed a format string literally: `console.log("%s and %d", "fmt", 5)` logged "%s and %d fmt 5" instead of "fmt and 5".
Fixed: a first string argument followed by more arguments takes them in its specifiers, as the WHATWG console Formatter does: %s, %o and %O show the value as console.log would, %d and %i parse an integer, %f a float, %c takes a style and prints nothing, %% is %. A specifier left without an argument stays as written; leftover arguments follow, space-separated; a lone string is printed as is. Both engines.
Scenarios: codemode-lang-errors.test.ts "console output formatting", codemode-stdlib-console.test.ts "console formatting"
Upstream tests: test/codemode.test.ts adds "a first string argument takes the arguments after it in its format specifiers"; interpreter-support.md lists the specifiers

diff --git a/packages/codemode/interpreter-support.md b/packages/codemode/interpreter-support.md
index 52ad4dc9c1..d81c760dff 100644
--- a/packages/codemode/interpreter-support.md
+++ b/packages/codemode/interpreter-support.md
@@ -469,6 +469,8 @@ reject }` object.
       number primitives, and ignore non-string/non-number entries. Primitive wrapper entries remain unsupported.
 - [x] Captured `console.log`, `console.info`, `console.debug`, `console.warn`, and `console.error`. An Error prints as
       `Error.prototype.toString` would show it (`Error: boom`), wherever it appears in the logged value.
+- [x] Console format specifiers in a first string argument followed by more arguments: `%s`, `%o`, and `%O` show the
+      value, `%d` and `%i` parse an integer, `%f` a float, `%c` takes a style and prints nothing, and `%%` is `%`.
 - [x] Captured `console.dir` and `console.table`.
 
